\subsection{Qualitative and behavioral recovery}
\label{sec:qualitative}
In the PushObj episode of \cref{fig:teaser}a, the native frozen controller brings the object nearly to its goal by replan 15 and then circles locally without closing the pusher error; ECP shares the first 15 plans, detects the stall, and completes the task two replans later (\cref{fig:push-distance}). Across triggered episodes, ECP moves the pusher $>15$~px from the stalled position within three replans \textbf{38\% of the time vs.\ 11\% natively}, and reduces object error or succeeds within ten replans in \textbf{34\% vs.\ 1\% natively} (\cref{sec:behavior}). In Maze, stagnation memory makes dead-end corridors costly and redirects the trajectory. Cross-domain examples show recovered PushT episodes and Rope recovery (\cref{fig:execution-recovery,fig:rope-main-rollout}).

