\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
\input{tables/world_model_comparison}

\subsection{Experimental setup}
\paragraph{Tasks and metrics.}
All tasks are specified by a goal image: \emph{PushObj} comprises 350 pushing tasks over seven object shapes, of which I, small-T, and square are unseen during training. \emph{PushT} uses 50 matched start--goal pairs under clean observations and six visual shifts: blur, salt-and-pepper (S\&P) noise, darker lighting, and a recolored agent, object, or goal. \emph{Maze} comprises 100 navigation tasks across five held-out layouts, and \emph{Rope} 20 deformable-object targets repeated under three seeds. PushObj and PushT succeed when the joint pusher/object position error is below 20~pixels and object-angle error is below $\pi/9$; Maze succeeds when position error is below 0.5~units. On Rope, we report final Chamfer distance (CD) and count success at $\text{CD}<0.3$ within at most 20 pushes.

\paragraph{World models and baselines.}
Our primary world model couples a ResNet visual encoder with a latent Transformer predictor pretrained offline. \emph{Frozen} holds all weights fixed during execution, whereas \emph{AdaJEPA} adapts them online from executed transitions~\cite{wang2026adajepa}. \emph{Released} denotes the published checkpoint, \emph{Data aug.} a checkpoint trained offline on our interaction-biased data, and \emph{Native} the planner without ECP. We compare against and apply ECP to DINO-WM~\cite{zhou2025dinowm} and LeWM~\cite{maes2026lewm} with fixed weights. \emph{Ours} combines augmented training with ECP on PushObj and PushT, and adds ECP to the released models on Maze and Rope.

\paragraph{Implementation details.}
PushObj and PushT controllers receive 150 control calls and retain their gradient-descent (GD) or cross-entropy (CEM) planner. ResNet controllers allow 30 replans, each executing five controls from a 25-control horizon; Maze repeats each control four times. Rope uses fixed DINO-WM with three pushes per plan and observation after every push. Across tasks, the detection window spans $W=3$ feedback steps (one completed replan on PushObj, PushT, and Maze; one push on Rope) after a task-specific warm-up. Thresholds satisfy $\gamma_{\rm lo}=\gamma_{\rm hi}/3$, with $\gamma_{\rm hi}=0.45$ for main PushObj and $0.15$ for PushT, Maze, Rope, and PushObj component studies. Memory uses $\beta=0.25$ and $\kappa=2$, and goal weight is multiplied by $b=10$ for three planning calls. ECP uses memory $S$ and proprioceptive reweighting $P$ on PushObj and PushT, and $S$ alone on Maze and Rope; Rope stores unit memory weights after five warm-up pushes. Paired comparisons with and without ECP share identical checkpoints, tasks, seeds, and execution budgets.

\subsection{Comparison with recent world models}
\label{sec:comparison}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{figures/fig5_execution_recovery.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{ECP recovers failed plans and reduces Rope error.} Brown/teal denote without/with ECP. In (a--f), labels give outcome and stopping replans (maximum 30). Rope pairs (g--h) show both methods after 20 pushes; CD is Chamfer distance (lower is better; success $<0.3$). ECP succeeds in (a--g) and lowers CD in (h), where both methods fail.}
\label{fig:execution-recovery}
\end{figure*}
\Cref{tab:main} compares complete PushObj and PushT controllers. \emph{Ours (AdaJEPA)} exceeds the strongest baseline by \textbf{+8.3~pp} on PushObj (77.7\% vs.\ 69.4\%) and \textbf{+7.4~pp} across the six PushT visual shifts (63.7\% vs.\ 56.3\%), leading 13 of the 16 columns. \emph{Ours (frozen)} also outperforms released AdaJEPA on PushObj (72.3\% vs.\ 69.4\%). ECP never lowers PushT success across the 28 combinations of checkpoint, update rule, and visual shift (\cref{tab:pusht-ecp}). Additional fixed-weight R3M, DINO CLS, and DDP-WM controls remain below both of our controllers on average (\cref{tab:extra-baselines}).

\subsection{Generality across backbones, tasks, and updates}
\label{sec:generality}
\paragraph{Other backbones.}
On LeWM with fixed parameters, interaction-biased training and ECP together gain \textbf{+18.6~pp} on PushObj over matched random-data training. Memory and pusher reweighting alone change success by less than one point; a triggered contact-directed term supplies the full recovery gain (\cref{tab:lewm-contact}).

\paragraph{Checkpoints and update rules.}
Applied to the released ResNet checkpoint without retraining, ECP gains \textbf{+16.0~pp} on PushObj with fixed weights and \textbf{+3.7~pp} with AdaJEPA (\cref{tab:components}).

\paragraph{Navigation and deformable objects.}
On Maze, stagnation memory raises success from 48.0\% to 50.0\% (frozen) and from 63.0\% to 66.0\% (loss-gated AdaJEPA) without losing a single native success (\cref{tab:maze}). Rope changes the dynamics, backbone (DINO-WM), and optimizer (CEM) at once. Across three seeds, fixed-strength ECP lowers mean final CD from 0.6865 to 0.4793 (30.2\%) and raises success from 55.0\% to 71.7\%. Mean pushes fall from 12.68 to 11.30, including unsuccessful runs (\cref{tab:rope}). Mean CD falls below 0.7 after 12 pushes, versus 14 for native planning (\cref{fig:rope-cd}).

